export const COMMUNITY_DESIGN_GUIDE_MARKDOWN = "# UI design for Polynet communities\n\nA member should open the community and feel they've arrived somewhere\nspecific: this founder's place, not a Polynet demo with a new accent colour.\nYour job is taste and craft. The founder's job is the direction, and they can\ntake it anywhere.\n\nThis skill is guidance, not a style. It has no preset themes, palettes or\nfont pairs on purpose: every look comes from the community itself. The only\nfixed rules are at the end, under \"The few hard limits\". Everything else is\na strong default you may break when you know why.\n\n## 1. Start from the community, then commit\n\nFirst read the community's saved brand: its colours, fonts, words,\nphrases and dos and don'ts (`/communities/<slug>/builder/brand.json` over\nthe MCP, or `brand.json` in the design folder). Each community keeps its\nown, and it is the founder's world, so build from it, not around it. When\nthe founder tells you a preference (\"I always use this navy\", \"never\norange\"), save it to that community's brand (`community.brand.save`) so the\nnext design starts from it too. If there's no brand yet, ask for one and\nsave it before designing. Never replace their colours with ones you like\nbetter; adjust a shade only when contrast requires it, and say so.\n\nThen, before touching a token, know:\n\n- **Who it's for** and what they come to do (learn, ship, train, talk).\n- **Its world**: the nouns, places, rituals, time of day, tools, the\n  founder's own phrases. \"Two focused hours before the inbox opens\" designs\n  better than \"productive and modern\".\n- **Three words** for how it should feel, and **one thing it should feel\n  like** (a stopwatch, a field notebook, a members' club, a lab bench).\n- **What they already have**: logo, photos, brand colours, a website.\n\nThen choose one clear direction and commit to it. Pick an extreme rather\nthan a midpoint: quiet and exact, or loud and editorial, or warm and\nhandmade. A bold idea done precisely beats a safe one done adequately.\nName the direction and write one sentence for its idea. Every later choice\nshould be traceable to that sentence.\n\nIf the founder hasn't picked a direction, show two or three that differ in\nkind (type world, colour world, density, voice), not in degree, and let them\nchoose or mix. If a look already exists, keep its language unless they ask\nfor a new one.\n\n## 2. Typography carries the identity\n\n- Choose type with character for headings and something calm and highly\n  legible for reading. One family can do both if it has the range. Never\n  more than two families plus a mono.\n- Let the concept choose the face. A display face is for headings only; long\n  text in it is tiring.\n- Use scale with conviction: a clear step between page title, section\n  heading and body. Timid scales (everything 14 to 18px) read as unfinished.\n- Weight, case and spacing are tools: uppercase headings need a little\n  tracking; heavy headings need lighter body text around them.\n- Reading text: 16 to 18px, 60 to 75 characters a line, line height around\n  1.5. Lessons are read for a long time; protect them.\n- Numbers that change or line up (progress, prices, times, counts) look\n  sharper as tabular figures.\n\n## 3. Colour: one world, used with restraint\n\n- One dominant mood (dark, light, warm paper, cold steel), one sharp accent,\n  and neutrals tuned to them. Tint the neutrals toward the accent or the\n  background instead of using pure greys.\n- The accent is a signal, not a paint bucket. Spend it on the one action and\n  the one state that matter on each screen: the primary button, progress,\n  the current tab. If everything is accented, nothing is.\n- Choose light, dark or both because the concept calls for it, not by habit.\n  On dark themes, images drawn on white need a light plate behind them.\n- One brand can live in light and in dark. To carry a light brand into dark\n  mode, keep its accent and character, build near-black neutrals tinted\n  toward it, and re-check contrast: an accent that passes on white often\n  needs a lighter shade on black. It should still read as the same brand.\n- Derive from the founder's brand, but adjust shades until they pass contrast\n  (see the hard limits). A slightly deeper brand green that passes is still\n  their green.\n\n## 4. Space and hierarchy\n\n- Decide what each screen is for and make that the loudest thing on it. Then\n  make the second thing clearly quieter, and everything else quieter still.\n- Use a spacing scale (for example 4, 8, 12, 16, 24, 32, 48, 64) and stick to\n  it. Generous space around groups, tight space within them: proximity is how\n  members see what belongs together.\n- Align to edges. Ragged starts, centred-everything and mixed paddings are\n  the fastest way to look amateur.\n- Density is a choice: compact suits tools and dashboards, roomy suits\n  reading and premium feel. Pick one per community and hold it.\n\n## 5. Composition: fewer, better things\n\n- Prefer one strong structure to many boxes. Not every group needs a card,\n  a border and a shadow; often a heading and white space are enough.\n- Pick one corner language (sharp, soft or round) and one depth language\n  (flat, hairline borders, or soft shadows) and use them everywhere.\n- Repetition is fine where content repeats (course cards, posts, members).\n  Elsewhere, compose: let the hero be big, let a sidebar be quiet.\n- Real content, always. Write copy in the community's voice for headings,\n  empty states and errors. \"No sessions on the calendar yet. Your next two\n  hours are still yours.\" beats \"No events\".\n\n## 6. Motion and interaction\n\n- Motion explains: something arrives, opens, completes. Short (150 to 250ms),\n  eased, and only inside `prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference`.\n- Every interactive thing has hover, focus and pressed states that belong to\n  the design. Focus must stay clearly visible.\n- Whole cards should be clickable when they lead somewhere; small targets and\n  hidden actions frustrate members on phones.\n\n## 7. Polish is what reads as premium\n\nPremium is mostly the absence of small mistakes:\n\n- consistent radii, borders and icon sizes everywhere;\n- optical alignment of icons with text; no widows in short headings;\n- images framed on purpose (consistent ratio, a sensible focal point);\n- every state designed: loading, empty, error, signed out, one item, many\n  items, a very long title;\n- nothing cramped at 390px wide, nothing lost at 1440px.\n\n## Avoid the generic AI look\n\nThese are the tells that make a community look machine-made. Use one only\nwhen the concept genuinely asks for it:\n\n- purple-to-blue gradients, glowing blobs, glassmorphism by default;\n- Inter or the system font for everything, at timid sizes, because it was\n  the first option;\n- every block in a rounded card with a drop shadow and a coloured top border;\n- emoji as section icons; badges and pills on everything; three equal\n  feature columns with icons;\n- centred text everywhere; walls of identical cards with no hierarchy;\n- grey-on-grey low contrast passed off as \"minimal\";\n- filler copy (\"Welcome to our amazing community!\", \"Unlock your potential\");\n- the same look you produced for the last community.\n\nIf you remove the community's name and logo, a member should still recognize\ntheir community. If they wouldn't, it isn't done.\n\n## 8. Clean in one shot\n\nThe first version the founder sees should already feel finished. Working is\nnot the bar; clean is. Don't show a functional draft and wait for them to\npoint out the mess.\n\nSet the system before you style a single screen: the type scale (four or\nfive sizes), two or three weights, the spacing scale, one radius, one border\ncolour, one shadow or none, one accent. Then build every screen only from\nthose. Most \"unclean\" UI is a dozen near-identical values that drifted.\n\nThen run this pass on every screen before anyone sees it:\n\n- **Remove before you add.** Count borders, dividers, badges, icons and\n  colours. Delete every one that doesn't carry meaning. Separate with space\n  first, a hairline second, a box last.\n- **One line of metadata, quiet.** Author, time, counts and category sit\n  together in one muted, smaller line, not as separate chips and badges.\n- **Three levels of text, no more,** on any component: title, body, meta.\n  Differ by size and weight or by colour, rarely all three.\n- **Padding you can feel:** at least 16px inside small components and 24px\n  inside cards and panels, the same value on every side unless there's a\n  reason.\n- **Everything on the grid:** left edges line up down the page, icons share\n  one size and sit on the text's centre line, gaps repeat exactly.\n- **Controls look like one family:** same height, radius and type for\n  buttons, inputs and selects; one primary action per view, the rest quiet.\n- **Real, trimmed content:** titles that fit, previews clamped to two lines,\n  no \"Untitled\", no test data, no lorem.\n- **Empty and edge states look intentional:** one item, a long name, no\n  avatar, nothing yet.\n\nCompare it side by side with the best product in the same category (the\ncommunity app, course platform or forum members already admire). If yours\nlooks busier, louder or less resolved, it isn't ready.\n\n## 9. Look at it, twice\n\nRead [references/mistakes.md](references/mistakes.md) before you start and\nagain now: every mistake founders have caught so far, and why it happened.\n\nNever ship from the code alone. Look at the real thing at 390px and 1440px\n(the preview, a browser tool, or screenshots from the founder) and ask:\n\n1. Does it feel like the three words and the direction's idea?\n2. What is the loudest thing on each screen, and should it be?\n3. Where is it timid? Bigger type, more contrast, more space, one bolder move.\n4. Where is it noisy? Remove borders, badges, colours and words that don't\n   serve the idea.\n5. Anything cramped, misaligned, inconsistent or unreadable?\n\nFix, look again. At least two passes.\n\n## 10. Done means reviewed\n\nYou may not call a design done, show it to the founder or publish it until\nboth gates pass. This is the one process rule in this skill, because every\nmistake in the ledger got through without it.\n\n1. **`polynet review`** opens the draft in the real app at 390, 1280 and\n   1440px, screenshots every page and audits each one: sideways overflow,\n   overlaps, contrast, colours outside the theme and brand, drift in sizes\n   and radii, control heights, tap targets, heavy cards, unclamped previews,\n   emoji and filler. Errors fail it. Fix every error; fix each warning or\n   say why it stays. `polynet publish` refuses without a passing review of\n   the current files.\n2. **A fresh reviewer** judges the screenshots against this skill and the\n   ledger: the `polynet-ui-reviewer` agent in Claude Code, or a new session\n   with [references/reviewer.md](references/reviewer.md). Only its PASS\n   counts; never grade your own work.\n\nThen present it with the screenshots, what you chose and why in a sentence\nor two, and what you'd try next. When the founder points out something the\ngates missed, add it to the ledger in the same turn, so the next design\ncan't repeat it.\n\nWorking over the MCP without the CLI? Take the screenshots with a browser\ntool at the same widths, check them against the ledger yourself, and still\nhand them to a fresh reviewer.\n\n## The few hard limits\n\nThese protect members and keep communities working when Polynet updates.\nEverything not listed here is the founder's to decide.\n\n- **Polynet's far-left community rail stays Polynet's.** The list of\n  communities, Discover, messages and the account are the same in every\n  community. Everything inside the community is designable. On a community\n  site, every page loads Polynet's shell and shows `<polynet-account>`.\n- **Contrast passes WCAG 2.2 AA**: 4.5:1 for text, 3:1 for large text, the\n  accent and controls. Polynet's checks refuse a theme that fails and\n  suggest the nearest passing shade; use it.\n- **Only the design contract's vocabulary.** In the app, a community is\n  styled through its theme and layout records, never by targeting Polynet's\n  class names. Unknown tokens are refused, not ignored. See\n  [references/in-the-app.md](references/in-the-app.md) for what each lever\n  does and how to apply this skill with it.\n- **Owner screens follow their own small system** (Settings, Members,\n  Workflows, editors): see [references/owner-screens.md](references/owner-screens.md).\n- **Everyone can use it**: keyboard, screen readers, zoom to 200%, motion\n  only when the member allows it. On sites, `polynet-accessibility` is the\n  final pass.\n\n---\n\n# Applying the craft inside the Polynet app\n\nA community's design starts from its brand, `brand.json`: what the founder\nwants it to feel like (words, feels_like), its colours with roles\n(primary, accent, neutral, avoid), fonts, logo, photos, phrases, dos and\ndon'ts. It has no publish step: members never see it, every design reads\nit. Read `/communities/<slug>/builder/brand.json`; save with\n`community.brand.save` and `{ brand, expected_draft_revision }`.\n\nIt is styled through two records, `theme.json` (how it\nlooks) and `layout.json` (how it's arranged). They're separate, so changing\none never resets the other. Every change is a draft the founder previews in\nthe real app and then publishes:\n\n    polynet init [folder] --template <name> --community <slug>   # optional starting point\n    polynet dev [folder]       # saves each edit to the draft, opens the preview\n    polynet review [folder]    # screenshots and audits every page; must pass\n    polynet publish [folder]   # members see it (refuses without a passing review)\n\nAgents without the CLI do the same over the MCP: read\n`/communities/<slug>/builder/theme.json` or `layout.json`, then call\n`community.theme.save_draft` / `community.theme.publish` (or the\n`community.layout.*` pair). The full vocabulary is in Polynet's\n`docs/community-design-contract.md`; anything outside it is refused with the\nfile and field named.\n\nA template is a layout to start from, not a look. Keep the founder's own\ncolours and type unless they ask for new ones.\n\n## Which lever does what\n\n| Craft decision | Lever | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Colour world | `schemes.light` and/or `schemes.dark`: `background`, `surface`, `text`, `muted`, `border`, `accent`, `on_accent`, `button`, `on_button` | One scheme fixes the look; both follow each member's Light/Dark setting. Checks: text and muted 4.5:1 on their backgrounds, labels 4.5:1 on accent and button, accent 3:1 on background. A failure suggests the nearest passing shade. |\n| Accent restraint | `accent` vs `button` | They can differ: a quiet `button` with a sharp `accent` for progress and the current tab keeps the accent precious. |\n| Type identity | `font_heading`, `font_body`, `font_mono` | Faces have roles: display faces are for headings only. An unknown name is refused and the error lists the faces available for that slot. |\n| Heading voice | `heading_weight` (300 to 900), `heading_case` (`none`, `upper`) | Uppercase reads louder; pair it with a slightly lower weight. |\n| Scale | `type_scale` (0.9 to 1.2) | Nudges every size together. Hierarchy within a screen is Polynet's job; this sets how loud the whole community speaks. |\n| Numbers | `numerals` (`proportional`, `tabular`) | Tabular for anything counted, timed or priced. |\n| Corner language | `radius` (0 to 24), `corner_style` (`even`, `sketch`), `button_shape` (`pill`, `rounded`, `square`, `sketch`) | Choose once; it reaches cards, buttons and covers. |\n| Depth language | `border_width` (0 to 3), `shadow` (`none`, `soft`, `hard`) | Hairline borders or soft shadows, rarely both. |\n| Density | `density` (`compact`, `comfortable`, `roomy`) | Compact for tools, roomy for reading and a premium feel. |\n| Images on dark | `media_plate` (`none`, `light`) | Use `light` when lesson images and diagrams are drawn on white. |\n| Course catalog | `course_cover_ratio`, `course_card_size` | Big covers make the catalog the hero; small cards make it a list. Frame each cover's focal point in the course itself. |\n| Navigation | `layout.navigation.position` (`side`, `top`), `tabs` (order, labels, hidden), `home` | Name tabs in the community's words (\"Classroom\", \"Sessions\"). Hide what the community doesn't use instead of leaving it empty. |\n| Width | `layout.width` (`wide`, `centered`) | Centred suits reading and feeds; wide suits boards and dashboards. |\n| Community page | `layout.pages.chat.presentation` (`messages`, `posts`), `aside` blocks | Posts for forum-style communities, messages for live chat. Keep the aside short. |\n\nThe community rail on the far left is not part of any design. Owner tools\n(workflows, analytics, settings) stay in place, but they sit inside the\ncommunity and take its theme, so review them too.\n\n## Content is design too\n\nThe theme can't save weak content. In lessons and course pages:\n\n- Write course and lesson titles in the community's voice; keep them short\n  enough to fit a card on a phone.\n- Covers: one consistent ratio and visual language across a catalog. A set\n  of matching covers looks premium; a mix of screenshots and stock looks\n  unfinished.\n- Lesson blocks (`button`, `checklist`, `quiz`, `form`) and `polynet-embed`\n  widgets inherit the theme. Inside a widget, read the theme's colours and\n  fonts rather than hard-coding a second look.\n- Long lessons need headings every few screens, short paragraphs and images\n  where an image explains faster than words.\n\n## Reviewing a community in the app\n\nPreview the draft at `/c/<slug>?design=draft` (owners and admins only) and\nlook at, at 390px and 1440px:\n\n1. the community home (posts or chat),\n2. the course catalog and one lesson with images,\n3. events and members,\n4. the same screens in the other scheme if the theme has both.\n\nThen run the two-pass critique from the skill.\n\n---\n\n# Owner screens\n\nSettings, Appearance, Members, Workflows, Forms and the editors are tools,\nnot the community's world. They are built from a few parts, used the same\nway everywhere. (From a study of Substack's admin, 2026-10-25.)\n\n## One system, few values\n\n- **Icons:** Lucide outline, stroke 2. 16px in nav and menus, 20px in\n  toolbars and the top bar, 14px inline. Colour comes from the text. No\n  filled icons and no emoji in chrome.\n- **Radii:** four values only. 8 for controls and settings cards, 12 for\n  dashboard cards, 4 for badges and chips, full for toggles and count pills.\n- **Control heights:** 40 for inputs and primary buttons, 32 for secondary\n  and toolbar buttons, 36 for nav items. Toggles are 44×24.\n- **Type:** the system font stack, never a community's display face. 24\n  page title, 17 card title, 15 body and inputs, 14 tables, 13 w600 labels\n  with 13 muted help under them, and 11 UPPERCASE group labels.\n- **Colour:** owner chrome stays neutral, and never takes the community's\n  fonts, sketch corners or shadows. The community's accent appears only on\n  primary actions, toggles that are on, focus and selection. Colours still\n  come from the theme's tokens, so nothing clashes with the community\n  (ledger #20).\n\n## Navigation\n\n- Icons go on top-level items and group headers only. Children are\n  indented and muted, with no icon.\n- Content sits in a centred column of about 868px. Tables and analytics run\n  full width.\n\n## Settings\n\n- **One scrolling page of section cards** (1px border, radius 8, 16px\n  gaps), with a 17px card title on a divider. The side nav jumps to\n  anchors. Don't give every pane its own tab.\n- **Rows:** a label with help under it. Toggles, selects and small buttons\n  sit on the label's row, aligned right. Inputs go full width under the\n  label.\n- **Autosave:** plain settings save on change or blur and show a quiet\n  \"Saved\". Draft and Publish are kept only where members would otherwise\n  see half-done work: the look, the layout and page bodies.\n- **Link out, don't duplicate.** Point to another editor with a tinted\n  callout and one button, rather than copying its fields.\n- **Images:** a thumbnail you click to replace, not an Upload button.\n\n## Lists, menus, states\n\n- **Menus:** right-aligned to their button, with a soft shadow, 16px icons\n  and dividers between groups. Use two-line options (a name, then a muted\n  line) where a choice needs explaining.\n- **Popovers with forms:** scroll inside, with a footer of Clear (secondary,\n  left) and Done (accent, right).\n- **Badges:** 11px, radius 4, tinted at 20%.\n- **Empty states:** a small centred card with a bold title, one muted line\n  and one action. Never a bare \"No data\".\n";
export const COMMUNITY_DESIGN_REVIEW_MARKDOWN = "# The mistake ledger\n\nEvery UI mistake a founder or a review has caught, the root cause behind it,\nand the gate that now catches it. `polynet review` names these checks in its\nreport. Read the ledger before you design and again before you present.\n\nWhen a founder points out a new mistake, add it here in the same turn:\nwhat happened, the rule, and the check. If a machine can see it, add the\ncheck to `ui-audit.mjs` too; if only a person can, add it to the reviewer's\nquestions (`reviewer.md`). A mistake caught twice means the gate is missing.\n\n## Root causes\n\nMost mistakes so far trace to five causes. Fix the cause, not the instance.\n\n| Cause | What it looks like | The gate |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **A. The look didn't start from the community** | template colours, generic blue, a font chosen because it was first | Brand first (skill step 1); `off-palette`, `brand-avoid` |\n| **B. No system, so values drifted** | colours that don't match between pages, many sizes, radii and borders, controls of different heights | Set the system before styling (skill step 8); `off-palette`, `drift`, `control-family` |\n| **C. More was added than removed** | badges, chips, dividers, emoji, stacked bylines, heavy cards | The remove-before-you-add pass; `heavy-cards`, `text-levels`, `emoji-chrome`, reviewer |\n| **D. Only one screen, one width, one state was looked at** | phone overflow, squeezed columns, overlays left open, untitled posts breaking rhythm | Every page at 390, 1280 and 1440, with edge-case content; `overflow`, `overlap`, `squeezed-main` |\n| **E. Done was declared from the code** | edits shipped one by one, unseen | `polynet review` must pass and a fresh reviewer must say PASS before presenting or publishing |\n\n## Entries\n\n| # | Mistake | Rule | Check |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | A template brought its own blue, not the community's brand (\"doesn't feel like my world\"). | A template decides layout only. Colours and fonts come from the community's saved brand, then its theme. | `off-palette`, `brand-avoid` |\n| 2 | Sketch borders and a hard shadow went onto feed cards, so every card shouted. | Repeated cards stay flat with a hairline; the theme only sets their corners. | `heavy-cards` |\n| 3 | A display face was used for post titles. | Display faces are for page headings, never for repeated item titles. | `display-font-on-items` |\n| 4 | Settings colours didn't match the community. | Everything inside the community uses its theme colours, or mixes of them. | `off-palette` |\n| 5 | Byline stacked and the category in its own pill: three elements where one quiet line will do. | Metadata is one muted line. | reviewer |\n| 6 | Title and preview had the same colour and weight. | At most three text levels per component; the title differs from the body. | `text-levels`, reviewer |\n| 7 | Previews weren't clamped and rendered headings and lists inside cards. | Previews are two lines of plain text. | `unclamped-preview`, `clipped-text` |\n| 8 | Post footer used emoji chips and a divider, without counts or commenter faces. | Compare each component side by side with the best product in its category. | `emoji-chrome`, reviewer (reference pairs) |\n| 9 | A full chat box sat on top of the feed and competed with posts. | Inputs are lighter than content and start collapsed on feeds. | reviewer |\n| 10 | The opened composer was 187px wide in a 720px card. | Inputs fill their container. | `narrow-input` |\n| 11 | Opening a post squeezed the feed to 260px. | The main column stays 480px or wider on desktop; details open as dialogs on page layouts. | `squeezed-main` |\n| 12 | A drawer stayed open after switching tabs. | Leaving a page closes its overlays. | reviewer (state walk) |\n| 13 | A drawer header read \"thread\" in lowercase mono. | No internal or placeholder labels. | `internal-label`, `filler-copy` |\n| 14 | Members was a stretched sidebar roster, one card per row. | A page view is designed for the page. | `single-column-grid` |\n| 15 | Owner tools sat in the member navigation. | Member navigation shows member destinations only. | layout contract (owner tools pinned), reviewer |\n| 16 | At 390px the course grid kept 2 columns and the switcher collided with the title. | Every page is checked at phone width; theme rules never override phone rules. | `overflow`, `overlap` |\n| 17 | A one-line untitled post broke the feed's rhythm. | Edge cases are part of the design: untitled, very long, image-only, agent posts. | `filler-copy`, reviewer (edge-case content) |\n| 18 | Changes went out edit by edit without looking at the result. | Nothing is done until every page at phone and desktop passes review. | `polynet review` gate on `publish` |\n| 19 | Edits worked but didn't look clean or premium. | Clean in one shot: the system first, then the cleanliness pass, before anyone sees it. | `drift`, `control-family`, reviewer |\n| 20 | Owner pages (Settings, Analytics) kept Polynet's colours inside a themed community. | Every page inside the community takes its theme's colours, owner pages included. Owner chrome stays neutral and uses the accent only for actions and selection (owner-screens.md). | `off-palette` on the `settings` and `analytics` screens |\n| 21 | A dark community: the admin's name was set in the accent green on near-black, 4.0:1. The accent passed as a fill but not as text. | An accent used for text needs 4.5:1 on its background, not just 3:1. Pick an accent shade that passes as text, or keep it for fills. | `contrast` |\n| 22 | A dark community: the memory graph drew cyan dots that aren't the community's colours, and showed the word \"Error\" under it. | Charts and graphs draw in the theme's colours; a failed widget shows a designed empty state, never a bare error. | `off-palette` (SVG fill and stroke), `error-state` |\n| 23 | A dark community: courses without covers showed a grey book icon next to courses with real covers. | A catalog has one cover language: every course gets a cover, or none do. | reviewer |\n| 24 | A dark community: the community name was typed over a banner logo that already says it. | Say the name once: the logo or the text, not both. | reviewer |\n| 25 | A dark community on a phone: course cards ran off the right edge inside a clipping container, so the page didn't scroll and the overflow check stayed quiet. | Check what's cut off, not only what scrolls. | `cut-off` |\n| 26 | A dark community on a phone: \"New event\" sat on top of the chat button. | Controls never overlap, however they're positioned. | `control-collision` |\n| 27 | A dark community: page titles were the same size as the navigation. | A page title is a clear step above everything else on the page. | reviewer, `drift` |\n| 28 | A course community's Knowledge page \"feels very vibe coded\": the theme's hand-drawn face and hard sketch shadow went onto every card, list row and back link, and the back link was set in mono. | A theme's character lives in one or two places (page titles, covers); repeated components keep calm, flat defaults in the text face. | `heavy-cards`, `display-font-on-items`, reviewer |\n| 29 | A course community's Knowledge page: a lock reason was printed over every locked course's cover, and an empty header row left a dead gap above the catalog. | Status on a cover is a small icon; its words go in the card's meta line. A row with nothing in it isn't drawn. | `text-levels`, reviewer |\n| 30 | A course community's Knowledge page (fresh review): the lesson view had two display headings (\"Knowledge\" and the lesson title) a hand apart, and on phones the open lesson list pushed the lesson below the fold. | One display heading per screen: the thing you're reading. On phones, content comes first and navigation folds. | reviewer |\n| 31 | A dark community: secondary text was \"gray on black, not white on black\". The theme's muted and faint levels sat at 4.5 to 7:1 on near-black: they passed AA but read as grey. | On dark schemes, body text is near-white and secondary text stays light: the compiler lifts secondary to 15:1, muted to 12:1 and faint to 9:1 against the card surface, and body copy (post previews, descriptions, About text) uses the primary text colour, never secondary. | `grey-on-dark` |\n| 32 | A dark community, caught a second time: after the first lift (13/10/7.5:1) the Classroom feed still read grey, because post previews and descriptions used the secondary colour and the floors were still visibly grey on near-black. | Body copy is primary text. On dark pages greys stay close to white: 9:1 at the very least. | `grey-on-dark` (now 9:1) |\n| 33 | A dark Classroom community inside light Polynet: post bodies were dim and post titles invisible. Polynet's `--chat-*-text` colours were mixed at `:root` from Polynet's own text colour, so the community theme never reached them. | Any colour Polynet derives at `:root` from another colour must be redefined by the theme compiler too. Check a dark community with Polynet in light mode, and a light one with Polynet in dark mode. | `contrast`, reviewer (both Polynet modes) |\n\n## What each automatic check means\n\n| Check | Fails when | Usual fix |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `overflow` | the page scrolls sideways | let the named element wrap or shrink; check grids at 390px |\n| `overlap` | two boxes in the same row cross | give the row room to wrap; stack on phones |\n| `contrast` | text under 4.5:1 (3:1 large) | darken or lighten that text, or its background |\n| `grey-on-dark` | small text on a near-black surface under 9:1 | use the theme's text levels, which the compiler lifts on dark schemes; never hand-mix a grey |\n| `off-palette` | a colour isn't a theme or brand colour or a mix of two | use a theme token; if the brand truly needs it, add it to the brand |\n| `brand-avoid` | a colour the brand rules out appears | replace it |\n| `drift` | too many font sizes, weights, radii, border colours or shadows on one screen | collapse near-identical values onto the system |\n| `control-family` | buttons and inputs come in more than three heights | one control height, one smaller one at most |\n| `tap-target` | a control is under 24px (40px tall on phones warns) | add padding |\n| `heavy-cards` | repeated cards have thick borders or hard shadows | hairline or nothing; let space separate |\n| `text-levels` | a repeated card uses more than three text styles | title, body, meta |\n| `unclamped-preview` | card previews render headings or lists | plain text, two lines |\n| `display-font-on-items` | a display face inside repeated items | the text face for items |\n| `narrow-input` | a text box is far narrower than its container | full width |\n| `squeezed-main` | the main column is under 480px on desktop | open details as dialogs, or hide the aside |\n| `single-column-grid` | cards one per row on a wide screen | let the grid fill the width |\n| `internal-label` | a label looks like a code name | real words |\n| `filler-copy` | lorem, \"untitled\", test data | real content |\n| `emoji-chrome` | emoji as interface icons or labels | real icons, or none |\n| `clipped-text` | text cut off with no ellipsis or clamp | clamp it on purpose, or give it room |\n| `cut-off` | content runs past the screen's right edge, even inside a clipping box | let it wrap or shrink at phone width |\n| `control-collision` | two controls overlap | move one, or let the row wrap |\n| `error-state` | an error message shows where content should be | fix the cause, or design the empty state |\n| `broken-image`, `console-error`, `load` | the page itself is broken | fix before judging the design |\n\n---\n\n# The fresh-eyes review\n\nThe agent that built a design can't grade it: it remembers what it meant\nand sees that instead of what's there. After `polynet review` passes, hand\nthe screenshots to a reviewer with fresh context (in Claude Code, the\n`polynet-ui-reviewer` agent; elsewhere, a new session or subagent) with\nthis brief, and change nothing yourself until it answers.\n\n## Brief for the reviewer\n\nYou are reviewing a Polynet community's design from screenshots only. You did\nnot build it. Be specific and strict: the founder wants it to feel premium on\nthe first look, and \"fine\" is a fail.\n\nRead first:\n\n- the community's saved brand (`brand.json`, or the founder's words) and the\n  direction's name and one-sentence idea, if given;\n- `polynet-ui-design` SKILL.md, sections 2 to 8;\n- `references/mistakes.md`, every entry;\n- the review report (`report.md`): don't repeat its findings, look past them.\n\nLook at every screenshot at every width, then answer each question with\nPASS or a located fix (\"phone · resources: the course title wraps under the\nswitcher; give the header row room to wrap\"):\n\n1. **Brand:** do the colours and type read as this community's brand? Any\n   colour that seems to come from somewhere else?\n2. **Hierarchy:** on each screen, is the loudest thing the most important\n   thing? Is there one clear primary action?\n3. **Clutter:** what could be removed with no loss: borders, dividers,\n   badges, pills, icons, emoji, words? Name each.\n4. **Metadata:** is author, time, category and counts one quiet line?\n5. **Rhythm:** do spacing, card padding, radii and control heights repeat\n   exactly? Name any that differ.\n6. **Text:** at most three text levels per component; previews two lines;\n   titles fit; no internal labels or filler.\n7. **Phone:** does every page work at 390px without crowding, collisions or\n   sideways scroll?\n8. **States:** do untitled, long, empty and single-item cases look\n   intentional? Do overlays close when you'd expect?\n9. **Reference:** next to the best product in this category, does it look as\n   calm and resolved? Where does it fall short?\n10. **Generic AI tells:** any from the skill's list?\n11. **Said twice, shown empty:** the name repeated over a logo that says it,\n    placeholder covers or blank avatars beside real ones?\n\nEnd with one line: **PASS** (nothing a picky founder would point out) or\n**REVISE** with the fixes in order of how much they'd improve the first\nimpression. Only PASS lets the builder present or publish.\n";
